86427863 #edb/ref 1071 "Find a $2\\times 2$ matrix $S$ that has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=5$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2,$ and corresponding eigenvectors\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186427863 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "403fa166-7921-5239-b399-2bac8767c7a0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186427863 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $B=\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\}$ is a basis of ${R}^{2}$ consisting of the eigenvectors of $S,$ then $S$ must be diagonalizable.\n\nSo, we can write $S=PD{P}^{-1},$ where $P$ is a matrix whose columns are the eigenvectors of $S,$ and $D$ is a diagonal matrix whose entries are the eigenvalues in the corresponding order:\n\n$$\nS={{[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{P}{{[\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\lambda }_{2}\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{D}{{{[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nLet $P=[\\begin{aligned}8 & 1 \\\\ 7 & 1\\end{aligned}].$ Then, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{-1} & ={[\\begin{aligned}8 & 1 \\\\ 7 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -7 & 8\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -7 & 8\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, carrying out the necessary matrix multiplication, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & 1 \\\\ 7 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -7 & 8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}40 & -2 \\\\ 35 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -7 & 8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}54 & -56 \\\\ 49 & -51\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186427864 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186427862 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186427864 #edb/ref 1069 "q-37605" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186427864 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186427864 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the eigenvectors of the $2\\times 2$ matrix $B$ given that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{aligned}]B=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186427864 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "44ed4b4e-a305-51c9-9d7d-957ba67e3cad" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186427864 #edb/ref 1074 "Notice that the matrix $[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ appears on both sides of the equation. Since this matrix is invertible (its determinant is not zero), left-multiplying the equation by the inverse gives the following expression for $B:$ \n\n$$\nB={{{[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{P}{{[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{D}{{[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $B$ is diagonalizable. The eigenvalues of $B$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2$ and the corresponding eigenvectors are the columns of the matrix $P.$ Computing $P,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P={[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{-1}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -5 & 4\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, the corresponding eigenvectors are: \n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487576 #edb/ref 1080 "[\\begin{aligned}17 & 15 \\\\ -10 & -8\\end{aligned}]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487576 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487576 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "057aa010-a2f3-5746-b90b-2f137e1f8b6c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487598 #edb/ref 1080 "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 6 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487598 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [