ef 1078 #edb/ref 17592186487647 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488173 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "fa3d1395-4451-583c-ab82-a35d131d2416" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488186 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488173 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488186 #edb/ref 1069 "q-140129" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488186 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488186 #edb/ref 1071 "Find a $2\\times 2$ matrix $C$ that has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=1,$ and corresponding eigenvectors\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488186 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "11fac3f6-18af-5205-bfbf-77c7cc7c000b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488186 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $B=\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\}$ is a basis of ${R}^{2}$ consisting of the eigenvectors of $C,$ then $C$ must be diagonalizable.\n\nSo, we can write $C=PD{P}^{-1},$ where $P$ is a matrix whose columns are the eigenvectors of $C,$ and $D$ is a diagonal matrix whose entries are the eigenvalues in the corresponding order:\n\n$$\nC={{[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{P}{{[\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\lambda }_{2}\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{D}{{{[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nLet $P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{-1} & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, carrying out the necessary matrix multiplication, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 12 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488262 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488155 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488262 #edb/ref 1069 "q-81214" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488262 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488262 #edb/ref 1071 "Find a $2\\times 2$ matrix $H$ that has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=2$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=7,$ and corresponding eigenvectors\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488262 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "cc5327fa-e153-5237-b670-7841684eaae5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488262 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $B=\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\}$ is a basis of ${R}^{2}$ consisting of the eigenvectors of $H,$ then $H$ must be diagonalizable.\n\nSo, we can write $H=PD{P}^{-1},$ where $P$ is a matrix whose columns are the eigenvectors of $H,$ and $D$ is a diagonal matrix whose entries are the eigenvalues in the corresponding order:\n\n$$\nH={{[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{P}{{[\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\lambda }_{2}\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{D}{{{[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nLet $P=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}].$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{-1} & ={[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -3 \\\\ -2 & -2\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, carrying out the necessary matrix multiplication, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}H & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7\\end{aligned}](\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 21 \\\\ 4 & -14\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}17 & 15 \\\\ -10 & -8\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488273 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488156 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488273 #edb/ref 1069 "q-37623" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488273 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488273 #edb/ref 1071 "Find a $2\\times 2$ matrix $G$ that has eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=6$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=8,$ and corresponding eigenvectors\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488273 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "deae4223-84c9-5248-9dcf-afbc4aa0a08d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488273 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $B=\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\}$ is a basis of ${R}^{2}$ consisting of the eigenvectors of $G,$ then $G$ must be diagonalizable.\n\nSo, we can write $G=PD{P}^{-1},$ where $P$ is a matrix whose columns are the eigenvectors of $G,$ and $D$ is a diagonal matrix whose entries are the eigenvalues in the corresponding order:\n\n$$\nG={{[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{P}{{[\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\lambda }_{2}\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{D}{{{[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nLet $P=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{aligned}].$ Then, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{-1} & ={[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{1}[\\begin{aligned}5 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, carrying out the necessary matrix multiplication, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}G & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 8\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}12 & 24 \\\\ 18 & 40\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-12 & 12 \\\\ -30 & 26\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488274 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488122 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488274 #edb/ref 1069 "q-140130" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488274 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488274 #edb/ref 1071 "Find a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ that has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=-3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=3,$ and corresponding eigenvectors\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488274 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e138229b-152f-5c0e-9e47-2d76a5b09b43" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488274 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $B=\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\}$ is a basis of ${R}^{2}$ consisting of the eigenvectors of $A$, then  $A$ must be diagonalizable. \n\nSo, we can write $A=PD{P}^{-1},$ where $P$ is a matrix whose columns are the eigenvectors of $A$ and $D$ is a diagonal matrix whose entries are the eigenvalues in the corresponding order:\n\n$$\nA={{[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{P}{{[\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\lambda }_{2}\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{D}{{{[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nLet $P=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{-1} & ={[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, carrying out the necessary matrix multiplication, we obtain,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}](-\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}6 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
